You're right about the unexpected errors, and the change is bigger than it needs to be.

**Unexpected errors.** The top handler, `report_unexpected_error`, already wrote a log line before it sent to Sentry. The one hole was its sister function `report_if_automatic`, which handles failures inside timers, callbacks and the app-start report. It sent to Sentry but logged nothing, and one line there (ErrorReport.cpp:131) closed it. That's the piggyback you describe. On the server it's the same: one line in the gateway's unexpected handler. So unexpected errors cost two lines, not 44.

**Expected errors.** Most of the 44 lines are for these: OpenAI out of credits, rate limited, unreachable, a sign-in refused, a printer that didn't connect. By design these never reach the handler, because the person is told a sentence instead. That's why the old log had nothing for the five failures I set off in the scratch app. What I did badly was put a log line at every place an expected error starts (the AI request code, the sign-in code, the printer code and so on) instead of at the few places one reaches the person. There are four such places, and every expected error ends up at one of them:
- `AgentHost::send_envelope`, which sends every message to the AI page;
- `HomeHost::send`, which sends every message to the Home page;
- the two JusPrin dialog functions in OwnDialogs.cpp;
- the handler itself.

One catch: an expected error is passed around as a code plus the sentence the person sees. It doesn't carry what the other side said (OpenAI's status, request id and text). That's why I logged where that was still known. The clean fix is to add a field holding those facts, which the page never sees. Then the four exits log everything with no line at the origins.

**Another group of my lines shouldn't exist.** These are failures nobody is told about, like a refused page handshake or a print report the server didn't accept. Under your rule, both ends are our own code, so they're unexpected. They should be thrown up to the handler, not logged where they happen. Logging them on the spot hid a reporting hole instead of fixing it.

**Decision needed: should I redo the change around the four exits?** Today the branch has 44 lines in 25 files, and it works: tests pass and the live run shows every failure in the log. The redo would be:
- the handler's one line;
- a log line at each of the four exits;
- the new facts field on the expected-error type, filled in where the AI service, sign-in and printer code create their errors;
- the swallowed failures above changed to throw to the handler.

I expect that to be about 6 log lines. I haven't checked yet that every expected error really passes through one of the four exits. I would check that first and list any that don't. The cost is roughly half a day and a fresh before-and-after run. Keeping the current change costs nothing now, but every new feature then needs its own log line, which is exactly what you're objecting to. My pick is the redo. Should I go ahead?

